WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:11.017
You ask an AI assistant which flag makes kubeconform, a tool that checks Kubernetes files, reject unknown fields.

2
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.775
It answers in two seconds, with no sign of doubt.

3
00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:15.651
Strict mode.

4
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.062
So here's the question we're shy to ask out loud.

5
00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:20.327
It sounded so sure.

6
00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:22.524
Doesn't that count for something?

7
00:00:22.524 --> 00:00:23.656
Hold your answer.

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.978
Back to the jam shop.

9
00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:34.768
A summer intern has read every cookbook in the library, and writes you a strawberry jam card in thirty seconds, in lovely handwriting.

10
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:36.003
It looks perfect.

11
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:40.280
It also says to boil the fruit at a setting your stove doesn't have.

12
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:43.545
And it names a brand of pectin that doesn't exist.

13
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:45.280
The intern wasn't lying.

14
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.308
They wrote what recipe cards usually look like.

15
00:00:48.308 --> 00:00:52.175
Neat handwriting and a confident tone feel like being right.

16
00:00:52.175 --> 00:00:53.915
They aren't the same thing.

17
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.707
An AI assistant works the same way.

18
00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:04.431
Inside it sits a language model, a program trained on a huge amount of text to predict the next words.

19
00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:06.343
It isn't looking anything up.

20
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:11.512
So common shapes come out well, because it has seen them thousands of times.

21
00:01:11.512 --> 00:01:19.112
Rare or recent details, a renamed flag, an exact field, a version, come out as guesses that look exactly like facts.

22
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.785
And it sounds just as sure when it is wrong.

23
00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:23.845
So its confidence tells you nothing.

24
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.425
Remember strict mode?

25
00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:26.307
It was wrong.

26
00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:31.871
The real flag is minus strict, and the tool's own help text settles it in seconds.

27
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:34.906
Now a real case from the lab.

28
00:01:34.906 --> 00:01:45.100
You ask an assistant for a Kubernetes Deployment for hello-devops, our tiny web app, with three copies, a health check and Redis as the database.

29
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.647
No files, no versions.

30
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.686
A Deployment is the settings file that tells Kubernetes how to run your app.

31
00:01:51.686 --> 00:01:54.836
The answer came back in seconds, neatly indented.

32
00:01:54.836 --> 00:01:56.635
How many mistakes are in it?

33
00:01:56.635 --> 00:01:57.664
Pause and guess.

34
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.458
Check one.

35
00:02:00.458 --> 00:02:07.233
kubeconform checks each object against the official schema, the list of allowed fields and their types.

36
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:11.575
With minus strict, it also rejects fields the schema doesn't know.

37
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:12.635
It finds two.

38
00:02:12.635 --> 00:02:15.547
The port is written as text, not a number.

39
00:02:15.547 --> 00:02:18.251
And timeout isn't a probe field at all.

40
00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:20.400
The real one is timeoutSeconds.

41
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.281
Without minus strict, the invented field slips straight through.

42
00:02:24.281 --> 00:02:28.997
A made-up field looks exactly like a real one, sitting where a real one would sit.

43
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.767
Fix those two, and kubeconform is happy.

44
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:32.557
Are we done?

45
00:02:32.557 --> 00:02:35.980
I know what you're thinking: the checker said valid.

46
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.757
Let's be suspicious anyway.

47
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:38.620
Check two.

48
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.836
A server dry run sends the file to the cluster's front desk, the API server.

49
00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:48.297
It runs every check of a real apply, then throws the object away.

50
00:02:48.297 --> 00:02:53.171
Here we run it against kind, the small practice cluster on your laptop.

51
00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:54.463
It finds one more.

52
00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:59.297
The selector looks for app hello-devops, but the Pods are labelled app hello.

53
00:02:59.297 --> 00:03:02.121
The Deployment could never find its own Pods.

54
00:03:02.267 --> 00:03:05.952
Some rules live only in the server, and a schema can't see them.

55
00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:09.235
That's why the loop doesn't stop at the first green tick.

56
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.596
Check three is you.

57
00:03:10.596 --> 00:03:13.554
No tool knows what hello-devops actually needs.

58
00:03:13.554 --> 00:03:18.337
So you read it, the way you'd read the intern's card next to your own stove.

59
00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:22.969
The probe asks for slash health, but the app serves slash health Z.

60
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.293
The image is latest.

61
00:03:24.293 --> 00:03:28.198
REDIS_HOST is localhost instead of the Service name, redis.

62
00:03:28.198 --> 00:03:30.251
And there's no readiness probe.

63
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.252
The draft also sets a CPU limit, which the lab leaves out on purpose.

64
00:03:35.252 --> 00:03:40.525
Neat, confident, and wrong in many small places, exactly like the jam card.

65
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:47.459
Paste the exact errors back, ask for a fixed version, and run the same checks again.

66
00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:49.869
This habit has a name: the verify loop.

67
00:03:49.869 --> 00:03:53.145
Draft, check with a tool you trust, fix, check again.

68
00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:59.185
Nothing that has failed or skipped a check reaches main, the shared branch that releases come from.

69
00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:00.819
Each check has a limit.

70
00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:04.436
kubeconform needs no cluster, but knows only the schema.

71
00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:07.988
The dry run needs kind, but knows your cluster's rules.

72
00:04:07.988 --> 00:04:09.990
Checks prove shape, not intent.

73
00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.444
Only your review knows what you meant.

74
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.085
Which gives a plain rule.

75
00:04:14.085 --> 00:04:19.013
Use AI for work you could check yourself, and never for a file you couldn't review.

76
00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:21.901
So the moral of the story.

77
00:04:21.901 --> 00:04:23.057
The model drafts.

78
00:04:23.057 --> 00:04:24.757
A tool you trust decides.

79
00:04:24.757 --> 00:04:27.477
And you decide what the tools can't see.

80
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.497
A quick check.

81
00:04:29.497 --> 00:04:33.659
An assistant tells you, with no doubt at all, to use strict mode.

82
00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:35.773
What do you do before you use it?

83
00:04:35.773 --> 00:04:36.797
Pause and think.

84
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:40.694
Check the tool's own help.

85
00:04:40.694 --> 00:04:44.757
Here, kubeconform minus h shows the real flag, minus strict.

86
00:04:44.757 --> 00:04:48.617
A few seconds of checking beats any amount of confidence.
